package android.hardware.radio@1.3;

import @1.0::RadioIndicationType;
import @1.2::IRadioIndication;

/**
 * Interface declaring unsolicited radio indications.
 */
interface IRadioIndication extends @1.2::IRadioIndication {
    /**
     * Report the current list of emergency numbers
     *
     * Each emergency number (@1.3::EmergencyNumber) in the emergency number list contains a
     * dialing number, one or more service category(s), mobile country code, and source(s) that
     * indicate where it comes from.
     *
     * Radio must report the emergency numbers that can be used to dial in the current
     * environments. Emergency numbers that are only used in other countries or cannot be dialed
     * at the current moment are not expected to report.
     *
     * Radio must collect all sources of the emergency number to build the indication. For example,
     * network operator signals, sim card information, modem/oem configuration, default
     * configuration, etc.
     *
     * Per 3GPP reference, defaultly, 112 and 911 must be always available; besides, 000, 08, 110,
     * 999, 118 and 119 must be available when sim is not present.
     *
     * Radio server must report clients the current list of emergency numbers, when any of the
     * sources resulting in the change of the current emergency numbers in the list, or every time
     * the client and the radio server are connected with each other.
     *
     * The reported emergency number list must not have duplicate @1.3::EmergencyNumber. Please
     * refer the document of @1.3::EmergencyNumber to construct each emergency number to report.
     *
     * Reference: 3GPP TS 22.101 version 9.1.0 Release 9
     *
     * @param type Type of radio indication
     * @param emergencyNumberList Current list of emergency numbers known to radio.
     */
    oneway currentEmergencyNumberList(RadioIndicationType type,
            vec<EmergencyNumber> emergencyNumberList);
};
